\pard\sa35\par
\pard\qc\sa150\i\fs18\cf6 Running desktop state. The live episode occupies the left side; worker state and decision context stay visible in the right rail. The lower panels contain saved evaluation evidence.\i0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The page is organized into five levels:\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\li620\fi-340\tx620\sa70\fs20 1.\tab Scenario controls define the policy, partner, workload, and team.\par
\pard\li620\fi-340\tx620\sa70\fs20 2.\tab Episode metrics summarize the current run.\par
\pard\li620\fi-340\tx620\sa70\fs20 3.\tab Order workflow cards show what work is ready, active, blocked, or complete.\par
\pard\li620\fi-340\tx620\sa70\fs20 4.\tab Worker and explanation panels show who is doing the work and the context behind coordinator decisions.\par
\pard\li620\fi-340\tx620\sa70\fs20 5.\tab Evaluation panels put the live demonstration in the context of repeatable research results.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 This hierarchy is intentional: the most frequently used controls and live state are at the top, detailed operational context is beside the workflow, and slower-changing research evidence is below it.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb280\sa130\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw12\brdrcf2\b\fs28\cf1 2. Header and scenario controls\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The dark header visually separates experiment configuration from simulation results. Every selector has a permanent label so its meaning remains visible after a value is chosen.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 \b Element\tab What it means\tab Why it is in the UI\b0 \par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Policy\tab Chooses the coordinator: PPO, Greedy, Static, or Random.\tab Makes learned and baseline behavior observable under the same type of scenario.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Partner\tab Chooses a simulated human behavior profile. Profiles are grouped as seen during training or held-out.\tab Lets a researcher inspect adaptation to different human speeds, fatigue, and error patterns.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Workload\tab Selects one, two, or three orders.\tab Changes task volume and deadline pressure without changing the page structure.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Team\tab Selects 1 AI + 1 human, 1 AI + 2 humans, or 2 AI + 2 humans.\tab Exposes coordination behavior at different team sizes.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Step\tab Advances the environment by one decision tick.\tab Supports slow, inspectable debugging and demonstrations.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Run / Pause\tab Automatically advances the environment or pauses it at the current step.\tab Supports continuous playback without removing manual control.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Reset\tab Starts a new episode using the selected configuration.\tab Applies selector changes and returns the simulation to a known initial state.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb200\sa90\b\fs23\cf2 Policy meanings\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 \b Policy\tab Behavior\b0 \par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 PPO agent (learned)\tab Uses the trained reinforcement-learning policy when a compatible checkpoint is available.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Greedy heuristic\tab Ranks ready tasks by urgency and dependency value, then selects the free worker with the best estimated completion time from observable signals.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Static workflow\tab Follows a fixed task order and round-robin assignment. It does not adapt to the current partner.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Random baseline\tab Selects uniformly from currently legal actions and represents a lower-bound baseline.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 All policies receive the same legality mask. Illegal actions therefore fall back to waiting rather than giving one policy an unfair conflict penalty.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb200\sa90\b\fs23\cf2 Button states\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab The primary Run button uses teal because it starts the main action.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Step is quieter because it is a secondary, deliberate action.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Reset is outlined to distinguish restarting from advancing.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Step and Run become visibly disabled when an episode finishes or reaches its step limit. A disabled button is unavailable, not missing.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb280\sa130\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw12\brdrcf2\b\fs28\cf1 3. Live episode metrics\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The six cards under the header provide a single-row health check on large screens and reflow into smaller grids on narrow screens.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 \b Metric\tab Definition\tab How to read it\b0 \par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Joint reward\tab Cumulative environment reward earned by the team in the current episode.\tab Higher is generally better, but use CES for a normalized coordination comparison.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 CES\tab Coordination Efficiency Score calculated from task completion, speed, synchronization, on-time orders, and conflicts.\tab Higher is better. The implemented theoretical range is -0.10 to 0.90.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Step\tab Current simulation step out of the maximum allowed steps.\tab Shows how much of the episode time budget has been consumed.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Tasks done\tab Completed tasks out of all tasks across all orders.\tab Direct measure of work completed. Each order contains six tasks.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Sync steps\tab Steps during which at least two workers were busy.\tab Indicates parallel human-AI activity rather than sequential work.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Conflicts\tab Illegal assignment attempts recorded by the environment.\tab Lower is better. The action mask normally keeps this at zero.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The CES formula is:\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\highlight8 CES = 0.35 x completion rate\line
\pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\highlight8     + 0.20 x speed score\line
\pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\highlight8     + 0.15 x synchronization score\line
\pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\highlight8     + 0.20 x on-time order rate\line
\pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\highlight8     - 0.10 x conflict rate\line
\pard\sa100\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 CES is shown as an em dash before the first step because there is not yet meaningful episode activity to score.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb280\sa130\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw12\brdrcf2\b\fs28\cf1 4. Order cards and completion line\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 Each order card is a compact operational view of one six-task order.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb200\sa90\b\fs23\cf2 Order header\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 \b Element\tab Meaning\b0 \par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Order number\tab Stable identifier for the order.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Urgency tag\tab Human-readable category derived from the order priority. Red indicates high urgency; amber indicates medium urgency.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 x/6 tasks\tab Number of completed tasks in this order.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Priority\tab Numeric weight used by policies and the environment to represent importance.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Slack / complete tag\tab Remaining time before the deadline, or a completed state.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Deadline\tab Step by which the order should be completed.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb200\sa90\b\fs23\cf2 The long line is task completion, not deadline progress\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The line directly below x/6 tasks fills from left to right as tasks complete:\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab 0/6 = 0% filled\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab 3/6 = 50% filled\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab 6/6 = 100% filled\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 Teal means active progress, green means the order is complete, and red means the order is still unfinished after its deadline. The deadline text on the right is a reference value; it is not a second bar and does not fill in the opposite direction.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 This separation prevents two different concepts from being mixed: completion answers \uhow much work is finished?\u, while deadline/slack answers \uhow much time remains?\u\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb280\sa130\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw12\brdrcf2\b\fs28\cf1 5. Task dependency graph\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 Every order follows this dependency sequence:\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\highlight8 Pick Item ------\\\line
\pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\highlight8                   > Pack Box -> Label Box -> Weigh Box -> Dispatch\line
\pard\li360\ri360\f1\fs18\highlight8 Fetch Box ------/\line
\pard\sa100\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 Pick Item and Fetch Box can begin independently. Packing becomes available only after both are complete; the remaining tasks then unlock in sequence. Arrows make these prerequisites explicit, and an arrow turns green after its upstream dependency has been satisfied.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb200\sa90\b\fs23\cf2 Task-node states\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 \b Appearance\tab Meaning\tab Why this color is used\b0 \par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 White with neutral outline\tab Ready to be assigned.\tab High contrast without implying ownership.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Pale grey\tab Blocked by prerequisites.\tab Visually recedes until it becomes actionable.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Ochre / amber\tab A human is working on the task.\tab Matches the human identity color used in worker cards.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Indigo\tab An AI worker is working on the task.\tab Matches the AI identity color used throughout the page.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Pale green\tab Task is complete.\tab Consistent success/completion signal.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The small label inside a node shows its current state: ready, blocked, the assigned worker name, starting, or done. The thin inner line at the bottom of a node is that task's own execution progress; it is different from the full-order completion line above the graph.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The fetch nodes are the same visual scale as the downstream tasks. On small screens, the graph keeps a readable minimum width and can be scrolled horizontally instead of shrinking the labels until they become unreadable.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb280\sa130\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw12\brdrcf2\b\fs28\cf1 6. Worker cards\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 Worker cards answer two questions: who is available? and what is their current operating condition?\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb200\sa90\b\fs23\cf2 AI worker\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Indigo dot identifies AI consistently with indigo task nodes.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Status reports idle or active task state.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Progress reports completion of the AI worker's current task.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Tasks finished is the worker's completed-task count.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab The profile badge indicates deterministic AI operation in the live demonstration.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb200\sa90\b\fs23\cf2 Human worker\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Ochre dot identifies a human consistently with ochre task nodes.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Speed is the observed normalized working pace; 1.00 is baseline speed or faster.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Fatigue is a normalized value from 0 to 1.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Error rate is the recently observed error frequency.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Status, Progress, and Tasks finished have the same meanings as on the AI card.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab A red zero-shot tag means this partner profile was held out from training.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The horizontal tracks make human behavior signals scannable without requiring readers to compare several decimal values mentally. The numeric values remain visible for exact interpretation.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb280\sa130\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw12\brdrcf2\b\fs28\cf1 7. Allocation summary\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The allocation card summarizes coordination behavior over the current episode.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 \b Field\tab Meaning\b0 \par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 To humans\tab Accepted assignments sent to human workers, with their share of all accepted assignments.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 To AI\tab Accepted assignments sent to AI workers, with their share.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Role switches\tab Changes between human and AI assignment types across accepted decisions.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Wait actions\tab Decisions in which no new task was assigned. Waiting can be correct when all workers are occupied or no task is unblocked.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Reassignments\tab In-progress tasks moved to another worker. Because takeover forfeits existing progress, a high count can indicate disruptive coordination churn.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The allocation summary is descriptive, not a target quota. A 50/50 split is not automatically better than an adaptive split suited to the active partner and workload.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb280\sa130\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw12\brdrcf2\b\fs28\cf1 8. AI decision explanations\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The explanation panel translates each coordinator action into operational language. Newest decisions appear first, and each record includes its simulation step.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 Each explanation contains:\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Decision \u what the coordinator did, such as assigning a task, changing its holder, or maintaining the current allocation.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Why \u relevant task availability, timing, or worker availability at that step.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Role logic \u why the selected worker type or unchanged ownership made operational sense.\par
\pard\li620\fi-300\tx620\sa70\fs20\bullet\tab Human trigger \u observed human speed, error, fatigue, or delayed-response signals relevant to the decision context.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The panel says \uObserved-input rationale, not causal attribution\u intentionally. It explains a decision using inputs available to the coordinator; it does not claim to expose the PPO network's private internal chain of thought or prove that one input alone caused the action.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 Green trigger strips indicate stable observed human behavior. Amber draws attention to fatigue, errors, or delayed responses that may affect assignment suitability.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb280\sa130\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw12\brdrcf2\b\fs28\cf1 9. Performance comparisons\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The comparison panel contains saved evaluation results, not the single live episode. It reports CES across 10 partner profiles and 50 episodes per profile so the user does not mistake one animation for statistical evidence.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 \b Comparison\tab What it answers\b0 \par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Static vs PPO\tab Does the learned coordinator improve on a fixed, non-adaptive workflow?\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Expert vs Novice\tab How does PPO performance change between a fast/expert and a slower/novice partner?\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Seen vs Unseen Partner\tab How well does PPO transfer to held-out partner profiles without retraining?\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 Each card shows both CES values as comparable bars. The percentage badge reports the right-hand result relative to the left-hand result. Positive and negative signs therefore describe direction, not automatically good or bad; for example, a novice score below an expert score is expected to display a negative difference.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb280\sa130\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw12\brdrcf2\b\fs28\cf1 10. Research evidence\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The research evidence panel turns the evaluated results into five thesis-level outcomes.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 \b Evidence item\tab Definition\b0 \par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Zero-Shot Adaptation\tab Held-out-partner CES relative to seen-partner CES.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Unseen Partner\tab Solve rate across the held-out-partner episodes, without retraining.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Coordination Improvement\tab Overall PPO CES relative to the static baseline.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Idle Time Reduction\tab Change in the fully idle step rate versus Static, measured across 500 episodes per policy. A negative displayed change means fewer idle steps.\par
\pard\tx2600\tx5300\tx7900\tx10500\sb65\sa65\fs18 Conflict Reduction\tab Change in disruptive in-progress task takeovers versus Random. Illegal conflicts remained zero for all legality-masked policies, so the card states this caveat explicitly.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\sa120\sl276\slmult1\fs20\cf1 The values come from results/evaluation.json and results/research_evidence.json. The saved evaluation and baseline tested tags distinguish measured artifacts from live simulation state.\par
\pard\sa35\par
\pard\keepn\sb280\sa130\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw12\brdrcf2\b\fs28\cf1 11. Completed episode\b0\par
\pard\sa35\par
